            0.239
          ],
          "content": "| x | - 2 | -1 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| y = x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 4 | 6 | 0 | - 4 | - 6 | - 6 | - 4 | 0 | 6 |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.448,
                0.138,
                0.551,
                0.157
              ],
              "content": "Table 2.1"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_table_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.251,
            0.424,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "If we locate the points listed above on a graph paper and draw the graph, it will actually look like the one given in Fig. 2.2."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.339,
            0.424,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "In fact, for any quadratic polynomial $ax^{2}+bx+c,a\\ne 0$ , the graph of the corresponding equation $y = a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c$ has one of the two shapes either open upwards like or open downwards like $\\triangle$ depending on whether $a > 0$ or $a < 0$ . (These curves are called **parabolas**.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.538,
            0.425,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": "You can see from Table 2.1 that –1 and 4 are zeroes of the quadratic polynomial. Also note from Fig. 2.2 that –1 and 4 are the x-coordinates of the points where the graph of $y = x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - { \\overline { { 4 } } }$ intersects the x- axis. Thus, the zeroes of the quadratic polynomial $x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - 4$ are x-coordinates of the points where the graph of $y = x^{2} - 3x - 4$ intersects the x-axis."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.431,
            0.258,
            0.896,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.619,
                0.735,
                0.705,
                0.755
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.2"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.79,
            0.901,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": "This fact is true for any quadratic polynomial, i.e., the zeroes of a quadratic polynomial $ax^{2}+bx+c,a\\ne 0$ , are precisely the x-coordinates of the points where the parabola representing $y = a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c$ intersects the $x - \\mathrm { a x i s }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.857,
            0.9,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "From our observation earlier about the shape of the graph of $y = a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c .$ , the following three cases can happen:"
        },
